    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d901">

                <pb facs="1702" n="1662" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v09p1/pg1662"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">110.11 DU/10–254: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 901<lb/>The Secretary of State to the Department of State </head>
                <dateline>
                    <placeName>London</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1954-10-02T23:00:00+01:00">October 2, 1954—11 p.m.</date>
                </dateline>
                <p rendition="#flush">Secret</p>
                <p> Dulte 25. At luncheon October 2 with Eden, we briefly discussed Suez Canal
                    blockade situation. We agreed on desirability clearing up the situation and Eden
                    asked that we consider possibility joining <hi rendition="#italic">démarche</hi>
                    in Cairo by our two Ambassadors. In general connection Arab-Israeli relations,
                    Eden reverted to his suggestion last April that the two of us should attempt a
                    Trieste approach, believing both might be ready for settlement, while continuing
                    unwilling to sit down same room together.</p>
                <p>As we rose from table, Eden said ruefully that when he was in opposition, he
                    suggested British send destroyer through Canal to clear blockade, a statement
                    which he had lived to regret.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed> Dulles </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
